WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.900
Good morning Hank, it's Monday.

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:03.820
I think we should do a theme week this week, and I think the theme should be open letters

00:00:03.820 --> 00:00:05.920
to our fifteen year old brothers.

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:09.620
By the way "Do you like my shirt?" is a rhetorical question, of course you like it.

00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:11.060
Dear fifteen-year-old Hank,

00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:14.060
Hi, it's me, John Green, your brother! I know I look old.

00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:16.580
15 year old Hank, life is a lot like pizza.

00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:18.060
Here´s a question:

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:20.780
Is pizza American?

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:26.140
Or is it Italian, or Greek, or Brazilian where they eat more pizza than anyone in the whole world?

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:28.880
More than 6000 pizzerias in Brazil.

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:33.620
6000 ! You can eat at a different pizzeria in Brazil every day for 18 years!

00:00:33.620 --> 00:00:35.940
Before you had to go to a place twice.

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:37.340
Unless you were eating pizza twice a day

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:38.820
which, let's face it, I would be.

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:39.740
So Hank, you're 15

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:44.420
and like a lot of fifteen year olds, you feel like when you are forced to read books in English class and look for metaphor

00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:48.520
and symbolism and theme and all that stuff, you are sort of being tortured.

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:50.880
By the way, fifteen year old Hank. I am now an author.

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:51.740
Huh?

00:00:51.740 --> 00:00:57.260
Here's what critical reading does Hank: it helps us understand what to think about and it gives us a framework for how to think about those things.

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:02.480
Okay, so let me give you an easy to comprehend example for what I'm talking about from this famously incomprehensible James Joyce novel, Ulysses.

00:01:02.489 --> 00:01:03.690
I'm getting to the pizza.

00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:09.600
So there's this Irish Jewish guy Bloom and he's sitting in a bar and one of the guys he's talking to, John Wyse, says:

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:11.480
"But do you know what a nation means?"

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:15.880
"A nation?", says Bloom, "A nation is the same people living in the same place."

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:21.440
And then everybody starts to make fun of Bloom for his stupid definition, and then Bloom adds: "Or also living in different places."

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:24.600
But then as the characters in the novel are laughing at Bloom, the reader

00:01:24.620 --> 00:01:27.940
realizes that, you know, we don't have a better definition

00:01:27.940 --> 00:01:30.340
for what constitutes a nation.

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:32.800
Or indeed what it means to have membership in any community.

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:35.200
I mean, Bloom is in this weird in-between space because he's Irish

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:39.320
but he's also Jewish, which many characters in the novel see as inherently un-Irish.

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:42.660
But ultimately we all want to feel like we're on the inside of our communities

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:45.440
whether it's Ireland or the United States or a high school clique or

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:47.580
Nerdfighteria. And the question of whether there have to be

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:51.820
outsiders in order for there to be insiders turns out to be a really interesting and difficult and complicated

00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:54.360
question, which is explored at length in Ulysses.

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:58.220
And thinking about, as the guys at the bar clearly can't, what we really mean when we talk about

00:01:58.220 --> 00:02:01.180
these identifiers, is a much better starting place for

00:02:01.180 --> 00:02:04.700
conversations about who is or is not American. Or who is or is not a Nerdfighter,

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:07.380
rather than the current method of discourse which is just to be like:

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:10.540
Ahhh I´m a Giant Squid of Anger, I do not listen to you!

00:02:10.540 --> 00:02:15.238
That form of communicating hurts not only the quality of discourse, but also the quality of my glasses.

00:02:15.238 --> 00:02:16.439
Which brings me to pizza

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:18.440
Hank, is pizza American?

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:22.260
Certainly there's a lot of pizza in America. Thank goodness.

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:24.520
Hmmmm, pizza

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:31.460
But pizza was originally Italian, although Italian pizza doesn't taste very much like this because this pizza is fortified with sodium

00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:37.260
which is a mineral, or a vitamin. All I know is that it's good for you. In fact Hank, pizza is so ubiquitous,

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:39.150
it's a little bit difficult to say what pizza even is.

00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:42.040
I mean, sometimes it comes without cheese, sometimes it comes without sauce,

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:44.080
sometimes it comes without a crust in a bucket.

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:46.760
And people are the same way, Hank. A nation is the same people living in the

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:54.480
same place or also in different places. There are Italians in Italy and there are Italians in America, and there are Italians of Chilean descent.

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:57.599
Everyone's laughing at Bloom in that bar, but we should be the ones laughing at them.

00:02:57.599 --> 00:03:00.418
They think there is some easy uncomplicated definition of Irishness.

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:05.660
But in fact, Hank, the fundamental thing that all critical reading does is reveal to us that there are not easy

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:08.440
definitions that distinguish "us" from "them".

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:11.220
Reading with an eye toward metaphor allows us to become the person

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:12.989
we're reading about while reading about them.

00:03:12.989 --> 00:03:16.360
That's why there are symbols and books and why your English teacher deserves your attention.

00:03:16.360 --> 00:03:21.760
Ultimately it doesn't matter if the author intended a symbol to be there because the job of reading is not to

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:23.680
understand the author's intent;

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:27.420
the job of reading is to use stories as a way into seeing other people as we see ourselves.

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:31.340
And when we do that, we can look out at the world and see a giant

00:03:31.340 --> 00:03:36.800
endless set of beautiful variations of pizzas! The whole world

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:40.680
composed of billions of beautiful delicious pizzas!

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:42.720
Hank, I'll see you on Wednesday.

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:46.960
Did I make that entire video so that I could eat one piece of pizza on my diet?

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:48.740
Maybe.

